>> title: Green Papaya, Peanut and Trotter Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork trotter, green papaya, peanuts, cantonese, old-fire soup, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 木瓜花生豬腳湯, muk gwaa faa sang zyu goek tong, muk gwa fa sang jue gerk tong, green papaya and peanut trotter soup, papaya pork trotter soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 45 min
>> slack: forgiving — a trotter cannot be overcooked in three hours or in five; this pot's only real deadline is at the shop, because a ripe papaya is jam by the second hour and a green one holds its pieces
>> step.1: Rich, and the pot made for a new mother — that is what it is for, and it is worth saying plainly rather than dressing up. The papaya goes in green and unripe; that is not a substitution for the ripe one, it is a different ingredient.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the trotter
>> step.3: rinse the peanuts and the dates
>> step.4: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: skim the fat, then season
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

Rich, and the pot made for a new mother — that is what it is for, and it is worth saying plainly rather than dressing up. The papaya goes in green and unripe; that is not a substitution for the ripe one, it is a different ingredient.

Blanch @pork trotter{2%lb}(900 g; split into sections by the butcher) from cold in @water{2 1/2%qt}(2.4 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{12%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every piece under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Rinse @raw peanuts{3/4%cup}(faa sang; raw, skins on, in dry) and @red dates{4}(hung zou; pitted) under cold water.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched trotter{} with @&(~1)peanuts and dates{}, @green papaya{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; muk gwaa — green and hard, peeled, seeded, in big chunks), @ginger{4%slices} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Skim the fat off @&(~1)soup{} with a ladle — a trotter throws a lot of it — then season with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
